\section{Mechanism}{


With \code{method = "regression"} (default): the raw score is banded by
quantiles on the reference data, the empirical log-odds of every band is
computed with Laplace smoothing in the orientation \code{direction} implies,
and a regression weighted by band size fits
\deqn{\ln(\mathrm{odds}) = I + S \cdot \mathrm{raw}.}
This absorbs sample reweighting, miscalibration of the WOE fit and prior
shift. It then composes with the PDO map:
\deqn{\mathrm{factor} = \mathrm{pdo}/\ln 2,\quad
      \mathrm{offset} = \mathrm{base\_score} - \mathrm{factor}\cdot\ln(\mathrm{base\_odds}),}
\deqn{\mathrm{score} = \mathrm{offset} + \mathrm{factor}\,(I + S\cdot\mathrm{raw}) = a + b\cdot\mathrm{raw}.}

With \code{method = "direct"}, the model is assumed calibrated: \code{I = 0} and
\code{S} is the sign of the direction (\code{-1} under \code{higher_is_safer}, \code{+1}
under \code{higher_is_riskier}), that is, \code{ln(odds)} is \code{raw} itself in the
right orientation.
}

\section{Odds orientation}{


\code{base_odds} is always expressed in the orientation \code{direction} implies:
non-event:event (\code{"safe:event"}) under \code{higher_is_safer}, event:non-event
(\code{"event:safe"}) under \code{higher_is_riskier}. The same word, \code{odds},
changes meaning between the two, and that is the most common sign trap in
the literature; hence the object records \code{odds_orientation} explicitly.
}

